Robert Abraham Lincoln Davis is Shannon's great-grandfather, the first husband of Anna Maud Woodruff and father of Alice Josephine Davis Jackson. His life traces a quiet but eventful arc: born in Michigan in 1865, he married Anna Maud Woodruff in 1895 at her father's home near Florissant, Colorado, and the couple settled in Mineral County — the mining county seated at Creede, where their daughter Alice Josephine was born and where William Thomas Jackson, the man she would later marry, was born as well. The marriage ended there too: a divorce on 26 July 1920, a date the family has long connected to the death, two years earlier, of their teenage son Robert Lincoln Davis in the 1918 influenza pandemic. Anna Maud remarried within weeks; Robert, by contrast, left Colorado for Missouri, reportedly along with members of his own family, and appears to have settled permanently in the McDonald County area by around 1923.

What happened to Robert in the more than two decades that followed is only now coming into view. His 1943 death certificate reveals a second marriage, to a woman named Cora, entirely unknown to his daughter Alice Josephine and her own descendants. No marriage record for this union has yet been found, leaving Cora herself something of a mystery — was this a quiet second chapter the Colorado side of the family simply lost track of, or something Robert kept deliberately separate from his earlier life? The certificate's informant, his sister Mary Hagerman, suggests the answer may lie with the Missouri and Arkansas branch of the Davis family, who may have known a great deal that never made its way back to Colorado.

A small but telling discrepancy surfaces in the record of Robert's own mother. Every document generated during her lifetime, and her grave marker itself, name her Mary Davis, née Cease. Yet her daughter — testifying as the informant on Robert's death certificate — gave her mother's name as Phoebe. The two are recorded together here as Phoebe Mary Cease, honoring the possibility that a daughter knew her mother's true name better than the public record ever did, while leaving the question of which name she actually used in life open for further research.

Family memory adds a human dimension the documents alone don't capture. Robert's granddaughter Josephine — Shannon's own grandmother — recalled that Simon Peter and Phoebe Mary were a mismatched pair: her grandmother was constantly at work, tending her garden, taking in laundry and housekeeping for others, and serving as the local mining community's midwife and triage nurse, all while raising a large family, while her grandfather's attention turned more readily to books and poetry. It was not unusual, Josephine said, for him to come home having spent his paycheck on a bag of books, telling the family that food for the soul mattered as much as food for the body.

<p>Two further documents close part of that mystery's edges, if not its center. A Colorado marriage record confirms that Mary Hagerman — the sister who served as informant on Robert's death certificate — was Mary L. Davis before her marriage to Samuel T. Hagerman on 29 December 1897 in Cripple Creek, and the 1900 census shows the couple's household intact three years later with an infant daughter, Rosalyn. That same Rosalyn appears again fifty years later as the informant on her mother's own 1950 death certificate, closing the loop between the two records. And in a small, quietly touching detail, both siblings' final arrangements were handled by the same funeral director, E. R. Pyeatt of Sulphur Springs, Arkansas — a thread of continuity between brother and sister that neither certificate states outright, but that the paperwork itself preserves.</p>
<p>The family's migration to Missouri was more circuitous than first remembered. In 1930, Robert was not with his son Frank — Frank's own family was still settled in Creede, Colorado, living just a few doors from William and Alice Jackson — while Robert himself was thirteen hundred miles away in Bisbee, Arizona, staying with his sister Rosamond "Rose" Davis Brown and her husband Thomas. By 1940, both branches — Robert and Frank's family alike — had regrouped in McDonald County, Missouri, with Robert enumerated as a grandfather in Frank's household. Which of them arrived first, and whether one followed the other or they converged independently, isn't yet known.</p>
